<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Paris Peace Conference · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Peace Conference was the meeting of the victorious Allied powers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, opened in January 1919 and dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the treaties of Saint-Germain, Neuilly, Trianon and Sèvres, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and redrew the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920.jpg" alt="Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noël Dorville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_Peace_Conference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
